{
  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Identify the type: find a %, increase/decrease, change, reverse, or compound",
      "Convert the percentage to a decimal multiplier",
      "For increase: multiply by (1 + rate); for decrease: multiply by (1 − rate)",
      "For reverse: divide by the multiplier; for compound: raise multiplier to the power"
    ],
    "title": "How to Work with Percentages",
    "content": "<p>A <strong>percentage</strong> means ‘out of 100’. To find a percentage of an amount, convert to a decimal (divide by 100) and multiply.</p><p><strong>Percentage increase/decrease:</strong> Use a <em>multiplier</em>. For a 15% increase, multiply by 1.15. For a 15% decrease, multiply by 0.85. This is faster than finding the percentage then adding/subtracting.</p><p><strong>Percentage change:</strong> \\(\\text{Percentage change} = \\frac{\\text{actual change}}{\\text{original}} \\times 100\\)</p><p><strong>Reverse percentages:</strong> If you know the amount AFTER a percentage change, divide by the multiplier to find the original. For example, if a price after a 20% increase is £180, the original was \\(180 \\div 1.2 = \\pounds150\\).</p><p><strong>Compound changes:</strong> For repeated percentage changes, raise the multiplier to the power. After \\(n\\) years at \\(r\\%\\) growth: \\(\\text{Final} = \\text{Original} \\times (1 + r/100)^n\\).</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>A laptop costs £480. It is reduced by 15% in a sale. Find the sale price.</strong></p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> This is a percentage decrease</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Multiplier = \\(1 - 0.15 = 0.85\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Sale price = \\(480 \\times 0.85 = \\pounds408\\)</p>"
  },
